er of the ellipse and F is one of the foci) from the center, two points are taken...\" So the two points are on the minor axis, each at a distance e from the center. So their coordinates would be (0, e) and (0, -e). But if the minor axis is the y-axis, then those points are (0, e) and (0, -e). However, since e is the distance from the center to the focus, which is along the major axis. So in standard notation, foci are at (\u00b1e, 0). So the minor axis is along y-axis, and points (0, e) and (0, -e) would be along the minor axis but at distance e from the center. But as I thought earlier, unless e \u2264 b, these points would lie outside the ellipse's minor axis. But in an ellipse, e = \u221a(a\u00b2 - b\u00b2), so if e is greater than b, then those points are outside the ellipse. But maybe the problem doesn't require the points to be on the ellipse, just on the minor axis. So perhaps they can be outside the ellipse. The problem doesn't specify that the points are on the ellipse, just on the minor axis. So even if e > b, they are just points on the line that is the minor axis, at distance e from the center.\n\nSo, coordinates of the two points are (0, e) and (0, -e). Then, from these two points, we draw perpendiculars to an arbitrary tangent of the ellipse, and we need to show that the sum of the squares of these perpendiculars is 2a\u00b2.\n\nOkay, let's start by recalling the equation of a tangent to an ellipse. For the ellipse (x\u00b2/a\u00b2) + (y\u00b2/b\u00b2) = 1, the equation of the tangent at a point (x\u2080, y\u2080) on the ellipse is (x x\u2080)/a\u00b2 + (y y\u2080)/b\u00b2 = 1. Alternatively, the tangent can be expressed in terms of slope. If the tangent has slope m, then its equation is y = m x \u00b1 \u221a(a\u00b2 m\u00b2 + b\u00b2). But maybe using the parametric form is better here.\n\nAlternatively, we can parametrize the ellipse using the parametric equations: x = a cos \u03b8, y = b sin \u03b8, where \u03b8 is the parameter. Then, the tangent 