ormula, this distance squared is equal to r\u00b2 - 25. Therefore:\n\n(dx*r + 12*dy )\u00b2 = r\u00b2 - 25 ...(5)\n\nSimilarly, for line CD, the distance from center (0, -R) to line CD is sqrt(R\u00b2 - (49/4)). The equation of line CD is ey*(x - 12) - ex*y = 0 \u21d2 ey*x - ex*y -12*ey = 0. So distance from (0, -R) to this line is |ey*0 + (-ex)*(-R) -12*ey| / sqrt(ey\u00b2 + ex\u00b2) = | ex*R -12*ey | / 1 = | ex*R -12*ey |. Squared, this is equal to R\u00b2 - 49/4:\n\n(ex*R -12*ey )\u00b2 = R\u00b2 - 49/4 ...(6)\n\nNow, equations (1), (5), (3), (6), and the unit vector conditions dx\u00b2 + dy\u00b2 = 1, ex\u00b2 + ey\u00b2 = 1. That's a lot of equations, but perhaps we can relate them.\n\nStarting with equation (1): 12*dx - r*dy = -13 \u21d2 12 dx = r dy -13. Let's solve for r:\n\nr = (12 dx +13)/ dy ...(1a)\n\nPlugging this into equation (5):\n\n(dx*r + 12 dy )\u00b2 = r\u00b2 - 25\n\nSubstitute r from (1a):\n\n[ dx*(12 dx +13)/ dy +12 dy ]\u00b2 = [ (12 dx +13)/ dy ]\u00b2 -25\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\n[ (12 dx\u00b2 +13 dx)/ dy +12 dy ]\u00b2 = [ (12 dx\u00b2 +13 dx +12 dy\u00b2)/ dy ]\u00b2\n\nNote that dx\u00b2 + dy\u00b2 =1, so 12 dx\u00b2 +12 dy\u00b2 =12. Therefore, numerator becomes 12 +13 dx.\n\nSo left side is [ (12 +13 dx)/ dy ]\u00b2\n\nRight side is [ (12 dx +13)/ dy ]\u00b2 -25\n\nThus:\n\n[ (12 +13 dx)/ dy ]\u00b2 = [ (12 dx +13 )\u00b2 / dy\u00b2 ] -25\n\nMultiply both sides by dy\u00b2:\n\n(12 +13 dx )\u00b2 = (12 dx +13 )\u00b2 -25 dy\u00b2\n\nExpand both sides:\n\nLeft: 144 + 312 dx +169 dx\u00b2\n\nRight: 144 dx\u00b2 + 312 dx +169 -25 dy\u00b2\n\nSet equal:\n\n144 + 312 dx +169 dx\u00b2 =144 dx\u00b2 + 312 dx +169 -25 dy\u00b2\n\nCancel 312 dx on both sides:\n\n144 +169 dx\u00b2 =144 dx\u00b2 +169 -25 dy\u00b2\n\nRearrange:\n\n144 -169 =144 dx\u00b2 -169 dx\u00b2 -25 dy\u00b2\n\n-25 = -25 dx\u00b2 -25 dy\u00b2\n\nDivide both sides by -25:\n\n1 = dx\u00b2 + dy\u00b2\n\nBut that's our unit vector condition, which is already satisfied. Therefore, equation (5) doesn't give us new information beyond equations (1) and the unit vector. So we need another way to relate variables.\n\nSimilarly, for line CD, let's use equation (3): 24 ex +2 R ey = -25 \u21d2 24 ex = -2 R ey -25 \u21d2 R = (-24 ex -25)/ (2 ey ) ...(3a)\n\nPlug this into equation (6):\n\n(ex*R -12 ey )\u00b2 = R\u00b2 -49/4\n\nSubstitute R from (3a):\n\n[ ex*(-24 ex -25)/(2 ey ) -12 ey ]\u00b2 = [ (-24 ex -25 )\u00b2 / (4 ey\u00b2 ) ] -49/4\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n[ (-24 ex\u00b2 -25 ex )/(2 ey ) -12 ey ]\u00b2 = [ (-24 ex\u00b2 -25 ex -24 ey\u00b2 ) / (2 ey ) ]\u00b2\n\nSince ex\u00b2 + ey\u00b2 =1, -24 ex\u00b2 -24 ey\u00b2 = -24. Therefore, numerator becomes -24 -25 ex.\n\nSo left side is [ (-24 -25 ex ) / (2 ey ) ]\u00b2 = ( (24 +25 ex )\u00b2 ) / (4 ey\u00b2 )\n\nRight side:\n\n[ (576 ex\u00b2 + 1200 ex +625 ) / (4 ey\u00b2 ) ] -49/4\n\nThus, equate left and right:\n\n(24 +25 ex )\u00b2 / (4 ey\u00b2 ) = (576 ex\u00b2 +1200 ex +625 -49 ey\u00b2 ) / (4 ey\u00b2 )\n\nMultiply both sides by 4 ey\u00b2:\n\n(24 +25 ex )\u00b2 =576 ex\u00b2 +1200 ex +625 -49 ey\u00b2\n\nExpand left side:\n\n576 + 1200 ex +625 ex\u00b2 =576 ex\u00b2 +1200 ex +625 -49 ey\u00b2\n\nSubtract right side from left side:\n\n0 = (576 ex\u00b2 +1200 ex +625 -49 ey\u00b2 ) - (576 +1200 ex +625 ex\u00b2 )\n\nSimplify:\n\n0 = (576 ex\u00b2 -625 ex\u00b2 ) + (1200 ex -1200 ex ) + (625 -576 ) -49 ey\u00b2\n\n0 = (-49 ex\u00b2 ) + 0 +49 -49 ey\u00b2\n\nFactor:\n\n-49 ex\u00b2 +49 -49 ey\u00b2 =0 \u21d2 -49(ex\u00b2 + ey\u00b2 ) +49=0 \u21d2 -49(1) +49=0 \u21d2 0=0\n\nAgain, an identity, confirming that equation (6) is dependent on (3) and the unit vector condition. Therefore, no new information here either.\n\nSo, in summary, equations (1) and (3) give us relations between direction vectors and radii, but we still need another relation to solve for r and R. However, the problem hasn't given us any other direct information. Wait, but we do know that the circles are externally