m asks for the roots in terms of x\u2081 and x\u2082, and we can't simplify this further without referencing p and q, this is the most precise answer.\n\nTherefore, the final answers are:\n\nFor the equation x\u00b2 - p x + q = 0: roots are -x\u2081 and -x\u2082.\n\nFor the equation x\u00b2 + p x - q = 0: roots are [ -p \u00b1 sqrt(p\u00b2 + 4q) ] / 2, which can be expressed in terms of x\u2081 and x\u2082 as [ (x\u2081 + x\u2082) \u00b1 sqrt( (x\u2081 + x\u2082)\u00b2 + 4x\u2081x\u2082 ) ] / 2. However, since the problem likely expects an answer expressed using x\u2081 and x\u2082 in a more direct way, but given the earlier example where substituting specific numbers worked, maybe there's a pattern.\n\nWait, take another example. Let\u2019s say the original equation is x\u00b2 + 2x + 1 = 0, which has a double root at x = -1 (x\u2081 = x\u2082 = -1). Then the second equation is x\u00b2 + 2x -1 = 0. The roots of this are [-2 \u00b1 sqrt(4 + 4)] / 2 = [-2 \u00b1 sqrt(8)] / 2 = (-2 \u00b1 2\u221a2)/2 = -1 \u00b1 \u221a2. So, the roots are -1 + \u221a2 and -1 - \u221a2. Comparing to the original roots of -1 and -1, there's no direct relationship other than the sum being the same (-2) and the product being -1. So, in this case, the roots are -1 \u00b1 \u221a2, which can't be expressed in terms of the original roots without introducing the square root.\n\nAnother example: original equation x\u00b2 + 0x -1 = 0 (p=0, q=-1). Roots are x=1 and x=-1. The second equation is x\u00b2 + 0x - (-1) = x\u00b2 +1 = 0, which has roots i and -i. But in terms of the original roots 1 and -1, there's no real relationship. So, this supports the conclusion that the roots of the second equation can't be expressed in terms of the original roots without using complex numbers or square roots.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\nThe equation x\u00b2 - p x + q = 0 has roots -x\u2081 and -x\u2082.\n\nThe equation x\u00b2 + p x - q = 0 has roots [ -p \u00b1 sqrt(p\u00b2 + 4q) ] / 2, which, using the ori