3} \\) to \\( x = \\ln\\sqrt{8} \\), we use the arc length formula for a function \\( y = f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{1 + \\left( f'(x) \\right)^2} \\, dx\n\\]\n\n1. **Find the derivative of \\( f(x) \\):**\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( 2 - e^x \\right) = -e^x\n   \\]\n\n2. **Set up the integrand:**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{1 + \\left( -e^x \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{1 + e^{2x}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute \\( u = e^x \\):**\n   - When \\( x = \\ln\\sqrt{3} \\), \\( u = \\sqrt{3} \\)\n   - When \\( x = \\ln\\sqrt{8} \\), \\( u = \\sqrt{8} \\)\n   - \\( du = e^x \\, dx \\) implies \\( dx = \\frac{du}{u} \\)\n\n   The integral becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{8}} \\frac{\\sqrt{1 + u^2}}{u} \\, du\n   \\]\n\n4. **Evaluate the integral using substitution and integration by parts:**\n   - The antiderivative of \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{1 + u^2}}{u} \\) is found to be:\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{1 + u^2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{1 + u^2} - 1}{\\sqrt{1 + u^2} + 1} \\right)\n     \\]\n\n5. **Evaluate the antiderivative at the bounds \\( \\sqrt{3} \\) and \\( \\sqrt{8} \\):**\n   - At \\( u = \\sqrt{8} \\):\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{1 + (\\sqrt{8})^2} = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\left( \\frac{3 - 1}{3 + 1} \\right) = -\\frac{\\ln 2}{2}\n     \\]\n   - At \\( u = \\sqrt{3} \\):\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{1 + (\\sqrt{3})^2} = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\left( \\frac{2 - 1}{2 + 1} \\right) = -\\frac{\\ln 3}{2}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Subtract the lower limit result from the upper limit result:**\n   \\[\n   \\left[ 3 - \\frac{\\ln 2}{2} \\right] - \\left[ 2 - \\frac{\\ln 3}{2} \\right] = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} (\\ln 3 - \\ln 2) = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\right)\n   \\]\n\nThus, the arc length of the curve is \\(\\boxed{1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\ln\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Given Function and Interval:**\n   The problem requires us to find the length of the arc for the function \\( y = 2 - e^x \\) over the interval \\( \\ln \\sqrt{3} \\leq x \\leq \\ln \\sqrt{8} \\).\n\n2. **Arc Length Formula:**\n   The formula for the arc length of a function \\( y = f(x) \\) from \\( x = a \\) to \\( x = b \\) is:\n   \\[\n   L = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1 + (f'(x))^2} \\, dx\n   \\]\n\n3. **Compute the Derivative:**\n   \\[\n   f(x) = 2 - e^x\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx}(2 - e^x) = -e^x\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (f'(x))^2 = (-e^x)^2 = e^{2x}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute in Arc Length Formula:**\n   \\[\n   L = \\int_{\\ln \\sqrt{3}}^{\\ln \\sqrt{8}} \\sqrt{1 + e^{2x}} \\, dx\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substitution:**\n   Let \\( t = \\sqrt{1 + e^{2x}} \\). Then, we compute the new limits of integration:\n   \\[\n   \\text{When } x = \\ln \\sqrt{3} \\text{, } t = \\sqrt{1 + e^{2 \\ln \\sqrt{3}}} = \\sqrt{1 + 3} = 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{When } x = \\ln \\sqrt{8} \\text{, } t = \\sqrt{1 + e^{2 \\ln \\sqrt{8}}} = \\sqrt{1 + 8} = 3\n   \\]\n\n6. **Express \\( dx \\) in terms of \\( dt \\):**\n   From \\( t^2 = 1 + e^{2x} \\):\n   \\[\n   e^{2x} = t^2 - 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2x = \\ln(t^2 - 1)\n   \\]\n   Differentiating both sides with respect to \\( t \\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\, dx = \\frac{2t \\, dt}{t^2 - 1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   dx = \\frac{t \\, dt}{t^2 - 1}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Substitute Back into the Integral:**\n   \\[\n   L = \\int_{2}^{3} \\frac{t^2 \\, dt}{t^2 - 1}\n   \\]\n   We perform partial fraction decomposition on the integrand:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{t^2}{t^2 - 1} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t^2 - 1}\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   L = \\int_{2}^{3} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t^2 - 1}\\right) dt\n   \\]\n\n8. **Evaluate the Integrals:**\n   \\[\n   \\int_{2}^{3} 1 \\, dt + \\int_{2}^{3} \\frac{1}{t^2 - 1} \\, dt\n   \\]\n   The first integral:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{2}^{3} 1 \\, dt = t \\big|_2^3 = 3 - 2 = 1\n   \\]\n   The second integral, using partial fractions \\( \\frac{1}{t^2 - 1} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{t-1} - \\frac{1}{t+1}\\right) \\):\n   \\[\n   \\int_{2}^{3} \\frac{1}{t^2 - 1} \\, dt = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{3} \\left(\\frac{1}{t-1} - \\frac{1}{t+1}\\right) dt\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\ln \\left|\\frac{t-1}{t+1}\\right| \\bigg|_2^3 \\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\ln \\left|\\frac{3-1}{3+1}\\right| - \\ln \\left|\\frac{2-1}{2+1}\\right| \\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\ln \\frac{2}{4} - \\ln \\frac{1}{3} \\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( -\\ln 2 + \\ln 3 \\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Combine Results:**\n   \\[\n   L = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{L = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\ln \\frac{3}{2}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\na) Show that if a number \\( a \\) is a root of the equation\n\n\\[ f(x) = x^{20} - 123x^{10} + 1 = 0, \\]\n\nthen the numbers \\( -a, \\frac{1}{a}, -\\frac{1}{a} \\), which are all distinct from \\( a \\) and from each other, also satisfy the equation.\n\nb) Show that if a number \\( \\beta \\) is a root of the equation\n\n\\[ g(x) = x^4 + 3x^3 + 4x^2 + 2x + 1 = 0, \\]\n\nthen neither \\( -\\beta \\), \\( \\frac{1}{\\beta} \\), nor \\( -\\frac{1}{\\beta} \\) satisfies this equation.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to solve part (a) and part (b) of this problem. Let's start with part (a).\n\n**Problem a)**: Show that if a number \\( a \\) is a root of \\( f(x) = x^{20} - 123x^{10} + 1 = 0 \\), then \\( -a \\), \\( \\frac{1}{a} \\), \\( -\\frac{1}{a} \\) are also roots, and they are all distinct from \\( a \\) and each other.\n\nFirst, let's understand what the problem is asking. We need to verify that for any root \\( a \\) of the given polynomial, these three other numbers are also roots. Additionally, we need to show that these four numbers (\\( a, -a, 1/a, -1/a \\)) are all distinct. \n\nLet me start by checking each of the transformations: replacing 