refore, \\( -\\beta \\) is not a root.\n\n**Step 2: Check if \\( 1/\\beta \\) is a root.**\n\nCompute \\( g(1/\\beta) \\):\n\n\\( g(1/\\beta) = (1/\\beta)^4 + 3(1/\\beta)^3 + 4(1/\\beta)^2 + 2(1/\\beta) + 1 \\).\n\nMultiply both sides by \\( \\beta^4 \\) (since \\( \\beta \\neq 0 \\)):\n\n\\( \\beta^4 \\cdot g(1/\\beta) = 1 + 3\\beta + 4\\beta^2 + 2\\beta^3 + \\beta^4 \\).\n\nNotice that this is \\( \\beta^4 + 2\\beta^3 + 4\\beta^2 + 3\\beta + 1 \\).\n\nCompare to \\( g(\\beta) = \\beta^4 + 3\\beta^3 + 4\\beta^2 + 2\\beta + 1 = 0 \\).\n\nSo \\( \\beta^4 \\cdot g(1/\\beta) = \\beta^4 + 2\\beta^3 + 4\\beta^2 + 3\\beta + 1 \\).\n\nBut from \\( g(\\beta) = 0 \\), we know that \\( \\beta^4 = -3\\beta^3 -4\\beta^2 -2\\beta -1 \\).\n\nSubstitute this into the expression above:\n\n\\( (-3\\beta^3 -4\\beta^2 -2\\beta -1) + 2\\beta^3 + 4\\beta^2 + 3\\beta + 1 \\)\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n- \\( -3\\beta^3 + 2\\beta^3 = -\\beta^3 \\)\n- \\( -4\\beta^2 + 4\\beta^2 = 0 \\)\n- \\( -2\\beta + 3\\beta = \\beta \\)\n- \\( -1 + 1 = 0 \\)\n\nThus, total expression: \\( -\\beta^3 + \\beta \\).\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\( \\beta^4 \\cdot g(1/\\beta) = -\\beta^3 + \\beta \\).\n\nBut \\( \\beta \\neq 0 \\), so \\( \\beta^4 \\neq 0 \\). Therefore, \\( g(1/\\beta) = (-\\beta^3 + \\beta)/\\beta^4 = (-\\beta^2 + 1)/\\beta^3 \\).\n\nFor \\( g(1/\\beta) = 0 \\), we need \\( (-\\beta^2 + 1)/\\beta^3 = 0 \\Rightarrow -\\beta^2 + 1 = 0 \\Rightarrow \\beta^2 = 1 \\Rightarrow \\beta = 1 \\) or \\( \\beta = -1 \\).\n\nBut check if \\( \\beta = 1 \\) or \\( \\beta = -1 \\) are roots of \\( g(x) \\):\n\nCompute \\( g(1) = 1 + 3 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 11 \\neq 0 \\).\n\nCompute \\( g(-1) = 1 - 3 + 4 - 2 + 1 = 1 \u2260 0. So \\( g(-1) = 1 -3 +4 -2 +1 = (1-3) + (4-2) +1 = (-2) + 2 +1 = 1 \u22600 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( \\beta \\neq \\pm1 \\), so \\( \\beta^2 \u2260 1 \\), hence \\( -\\beta^2 +1 \u22600 \\). Therefore, \\( g(1/\\beta) \u22600 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( 1/\\beta \\) is not a root.\n\n**Step 3: Check if \\( -