ability that Dima has n heads given that Mitya and Vanya have the same number of heads.\n\nBut is Dima's coin flips independent of Mitya and Vanya's? The problem says \"each flipped a coin 2n times,\" so I assume all three are independent. Therefore, Dima's flips are independent of Mitya and Vanya's. Therefore, the conditioning on Mitya and Vanya having the same number of heads might not affect Dima's probability. But for Mitya's probability, since we're conditioning on Mitya and Vanya having the same number of heads, that might affect the probability that Mitya has n heads.\n\nLet me formalize this.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let X be the number of heads Mitya got.\n\n- Let Y be the number of heads Vanya got.\n\n- Let Z be the number of heads Dima got.\n\nWe are given that X = Y. We need to compare P(Z = n | X = Y) versus P(X = n | X = Y). Since Z is independent of X and Y, then maybe P(Z = n | X = Y) is just P(Z = n) because of independence. But is that right? Wait, but the conditioning is on X = Y. If X and Y are dependent, then even if Z is independent, does conditioning on X = Y affect the probability of Z? Wait, no, since Z is independent of both X and Y, then P(Z = n | X = Y) should equal P(Z = n). So that would be C(2n, n)*(1/2)^{2n}.\n\nOn the other hand, P(X = n | X = Y) is the probability that Mitya has n heads given that Mitya and Vanya have the same number. So, that's a conditional probability where we have to compute P(X = n and X = Y) divided by P(X = Y).\n\nSo, let's compute both probabilities.\n\nFirst, P(Z = n | X = Y) = P(Z = n) = C(2n, n)*(1/2)^{2n}.\n\nSecond, P(X = n | X = Y) = P(X = n and Y = n) / P(X = Y). Since X and Y are independent, P(X = n and Y = n) = [C(2n, n)*(1/2)^{2n}]^2. Then, P(X = Y) is the sum over k=0 to 2n of P(X = k and Y = k) = sum_{k=0}^{2n} [C(2n, k)*(1/2)^{2n}]^2. Therefore, P(X = n | X = Y) = [C(2n, n)^2] / [sum_{k=0}^{2n} C(2n, k)^2].\n\nSo, we need to compare C(2n, n)*(1/2)^{2n} versus [C(2n, n)^2] / [sum_{k=0}^{2n} C(2n, 