ultiplicity 2.\n\nGiven the polynomial:\n\\[\n0 = 4x^{10} - 7x^9 + 5x^8 - 8x^7 + 12x^6 - 12x^5 + 12x^4 - 8x^3 + 5x^2 - 7x + 4\n\\]\n\nWe use Vieta's formulas to find the sum of squares of all roots (with multiplicity). The sum of the roots is given by \\(-\\frac{-7}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}\\), and the sum of the product of roots taken two at a time is \\(\\frac{5}{4}\\). The sum of squares of all roots is then:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)^2 - 2 \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) = \\frac{49}{16} - \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{49}{16} - \\frac{40}{16} = \\frac{9}{16}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x = 1\\) is a double root, we subtract its contribution once to get the sum of squares of distinct roots:\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{16} - 1 = \\frac{9}{16} - \\frac{16}{16} = -\\frac{7}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the squares of all distinct complex numbers \\(x\\) satisfying the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\dfrac{7}{16}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Define the Polynomial**:\n   Denote the given polynomial by \n   \\[\n   P(x) = 4 x^{10} - 7 x^{9} + 5 x^{8} - 8 x^{7} + 12 x^{6} - 12 x^{5} + 12 x^{4} - 8 x^{3} + 5 x^{2} - 7 x + 4.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Observation of Coefficients**:\n   Observe that the polynomial's coefficients satisfy certain equalities:\n   \\[\n   4+8 = 7+5 = 12.\n   \\]\n   Notice that the terms \\(12 x^{6} - 12 x^{5} + 12 x^{4}\\) are the leading terms of \\(12 \\Phi_{14}(x)\\), where \\(\\Phi_{14}(x)\\) is the 14th cyclotomic polynomial, suggesting that \\(\\omega\\), a 14th root of unity, may play a role in the factorization of \\(P(x)\\).\n\n3. **Evaluate \\(P(x)\\) at the Primitive 14th Root of Unity**:\n   Let \\(\\omega\\) be a primitive 14th root of unity such that \\(\\omega^{7} = -1\\). Evaluate \\(P(\\omega)\\):\n   \\[\n   4\\omega^{10} - 7\\omega^{9} + 5\\omega^{8} - 8\\omega^{7} + 12\\omega^{6} - 12\\omega^{5} + 12\\omega^{4} - 8\\omega^{3} + 5\\omega^{2} - 7\\omega + 4.\n   \\]\n   Using \\(\\omega^{7} = -1\\):\n   \\[\n   4(-\\omega^3) - 7(\\omega^2) + 5(-1) - 8(-\\omega^3) + 12(1) - 12(\\omega) + 12(-\\omega^3) - 8(\\omega^2) + 5(1) - 7(\\omega) + 4.\n   \\]\n   Simplify the above expression:\n   \\[\n   -4\\omega^3 - 7\\omega^2 - 5 - 8(\\omega^3) + 12 - 12(\\omega) + 12(-\\omega^3) - 8(\\omega^2) + 5 - 7(\\omega) + 4.\n   \\]\n   Recognizing contributions:\n   \\[\n   = 12\\Phi_{14}(\\omega) = 0.\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \n   \\[\n   P(x) = \\Phi_{14}(x) \\left(4 x^{4} - 3 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} - 3 x + 4 \\right).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Analyze the Second Polynomial**:\n   The second polynomial is symmetric:\n   \\[\n   4 x^{4} - 3 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} - 3 x + 4 = 0.\n   \\]\n   Use the substitution \\( y = x + \\frac{1}{x} \\):\n   \\[\n   4(x + \\frac{1}{x})^2 - 3(x + \\frac{1}{x}) - 10 = 0.\n   \\]\n   Let \\(y = x + \\frac{1}{x}\\), then the quadratic equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   4y^2 - 3y - 10 = 0.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solving the Quadratic Equation**:\n   Solve for \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 160}}{8} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{169}}{8} = \\frac{3 \\pm 13}{8}.\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the solutions are:\n   \\[\n   y = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -\\frac{5}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Replace \\(y\\) with \\( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\)**:\n   Solve the equations \\( x + \\frac{1}{x} = 2 \\) and \\( x + \\frac{1}{x} = -\\frac{5}{4} \\).\n\n7. **Solutions for \\(x\\)**:\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{1}{x} = 2 \\implies x = 1 \\text{ (double root)}.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x + \\frac{1}{x} = -\\frac{5}{4}.\n   \\]\n   Solve the quadratic \\( x^2 + \\frac{5}{4}x + 1 = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-\\frac{5}{4} \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)^2 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-5 \\pm i \\sqrt{39}}{8}.\n   \\]\n   So, the roots are \\( x = \\frac{-5 \\pm i \\sqrt{39}}{8} \\).\n\n8. **Sum of Squares of All Distinct Complex Roots**:\n   The roots of the polynomial include primitive 14th roots of unity and the roots \\(1, \\frac{-5 \\pm i \\sqrt{39}}{8}\\).\n    \n   The sum of squares of the roots of unity (including \\(1\\)) is:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{14} \\