n- y=1: x=0, total=0*1 +1*3 +2*5=0 +3 +10=13\n\nCase4: z=3 (three rings on C)\nThen y=0, x=0\n- total=0*1 +0*3 +3*5=15\n\nNow let's list all these totals:\n\nFrom case1: 3,5,7,9\n\nCase2:7,9,11\n\nCase3:11,13\n\nCase4:15\n\nSo compiling all these: 3,5,7,9,11,13,15\n\nWait, but hold on, that's 7 different totals. But the answer options include 7, but also 10,13, etc. Wait, the answer options are (A)12, (B)7, (C)10, (D)13, (E)6.\n\nSo according to this, the answer would be 7, which is option B. But wait, let me check again because maybe I missed some totals.\n\nWait, in case1, the totals are 3,5,7,9.\n\nCase2 adds 7,9,11. But 7 and 9 were already in case1, so unique totals added here are 11.\n\nCase3 adds 11 and 13. 11 was already in case2, so adds 13.\n\nCase4 adds 15.\n\nSo unique totals: 3,5,7,9,11,13,15. That's 7 distinct totals. Hmm. So the answer would be B)7.\n\nBut wait, the options have 13 as an option too, but D)13. Wait, but 13 is one of the totals. But the question is how many different point totals are possible. So 3,5,7,9,11,13,15: seven different totals. So answer is 7, which is B.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn't miss any possible totals. Let me verify each case.\n\nAnother approach: since each ring can contribute 1,3, or5, and we have three rings, the possible total points can be considered as the sum of three numbers each from {1,3,5}. Let's list all possible combinations.\n\nBut since the order doesn't matter, we can think in terms of combinations with repetition. Let's consider the number of rings on each peg:\n\nFor three rings, possible distributions (x,y,z) where x+y+z=3.\n\nEach distribution gives a total of 1x + 3y +5z.\n\nSo, let's list all possible (x,y,z):\n\nStart with z=0:\n\n- (3,0,0): total=3*1=3\n\n- (2,1,0): total=2*1 +1*3=5\n\n- (1,2,0): total=1*1 +2*3=7\n\n- (0,3,0): total=0 +3*3=9\n\nz=1:\n\n- (2,0,1): 2*1 +1*5=7\n\n- (1,1,1):1*1 +1*3 +1*5=9\n\n- (0,2,1):0 +2*3 +1*5=11\n\nz=2:\n\n- (1,0,2):1*1 +2*5=11\n\n- (0,1,2):0 +1*3 +2*5