es, then AD is perpendicular to AB, and FN is perpendicular to BF. So AD and FN are both vertical (if AB and BF are horizontal). Then points D and N are at heights equal to their respective squares.\n\nBut the key insight is that the distances DS and SN are both equal to the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs a and b, hence equal. Therefore, regardless of the specific values of a and b, as long as the squares are constructed on AB and BF, the distances will be equal.\n\nAlternatively, using complex numbers. Let me assign complex numbers to the points. Let B be 0, A be -a, F be b. Then D is -a + ai, N is b + bi. The circle through D, B, N. Find another intersection S with the real axis (line l). The other intersection is S = b - a, as found earlier. Then compute |S - D| and |N - S|.\n\n|S - D| = |(b - a) - (-a + ai)| = |b - a + a - ai| = |b - ai| = sqrt(b\u00b2 + a\u00b2)\n\n|N - S| = |(b + bi) - (b - a)| = |a + bi| = sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nSame result. So regardless of the method, coordinate, vector, complex numbers, the distances are equal.\n\nTherefore, the answer is proven. The key was setting coordinates and computing the distances, which turned out equal.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{DS = SN}", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( DS = SN \\) given the geometric setup, we start by assigning coordinates to the points involved. We place line \\( l \\) as the x-axis and set point \\( B \\) at the origin \\((0,0)\\). \n\n- Points \\( A \\) and \\( F \\) are on the x-axis with \\( B \\) between them. Let \\( A \\) be at \\((-a, 0)\\) and \\( F \\) be at \\((b, 0)\\).\n- Squares \\( ABCD \\) and \\( BFNT \\) are constructed on the same side of line \\( l \\). Thus, point \\( D \\) (from square \\( ABCD \\)) is at \\((-a, a)\\) and point \\( N \\) (from square \\( BFNT \\)) is at \\((b, b)\\).\n\nNext, we determine the equation of the circle passing through points \\( D \\), \\( B \\), and \\( N \\). The general equation of a circle is \\( x^2 + y^2 + Dx 