site angles inside the cyclic quadrilateral sum to $180^\\circ$:\n   \\[\n   \\angle SDB + \\angle SNB = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \n4. **Angle Calculations**:\n   Since $BN$ and $DB$ are diagonals of the squares:\n   \\[\n   \\angle NBF = 45^\\circ \n   \\]\n   Therefore, in the cyclic quadrilateral:\n   \\[\n   \\angle SDB = 180^\\circ - \\angle NBF = 180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n5. **Inspection of Inscribed Angles**:\n   Since $D$, $B$, and $S$ lie on the circumference of the circle, the inscribed angles $\\angle DBS$ and $\\angle DNS$ subtend the same arc:\n   \\[\n   \\angle DBS = \\angle DNS = 45^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n6. **Isosceles Triangle**:\n   In triangle $DSN$, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle SDN = \\angle DNS \n   \\]\n   This implies triangle $DSN$ is isosceles.\n\n7. **Equidistant Sides**:\n   For triangle $DSN$ being isosceles:\n   \\[\n   DS = SN\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{DS = SN}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Two fixed circles intersect at points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\). A moving circle \\(\\odot \\Omega_{1}\\) and \\(\\odot \\Omega_{2}\\) are externally tangent at \\( K \\). Each moving circle is internally tangent to one fixed circle and externally tangent to the other fixed circle. Prove that \\( K \\) lies on one of the fixed circles.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the point K, where the two moving circles \u03a9\u2081 and \u03a9\u2082 are externally tangent, lies on one of the fixed circles. Let me start by understanding the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, there are two fixed circles that intersect at points A and B. Let's call them Circle C\u2081 and Circle C\u2082 with centers O\u2081 and O\u2082 respectively. Then there are two moving circles, \u03a9\u2081 and \u03a9\u2082, which are externally tangent at point K. Each moving circle is internally tangent to one fixed circle and externally tangent to the other. So, \u03a