es": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A stone is thrown vertically upward with an initial velocity $V$. Neglecting air resistance and assuming the acceleration due to gravity is $10 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^{2}$, determine for what values of $V$ all moments of reaching the height of 10 meters will lie between: A) the first and second seconds after the start of the movement; B) the second and fourth seconds after the start of the movement.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out for parts A and B the initial velocity values V such that all moments when the stone reaches 10 meters are within the specified time intervals. Hmm, let me start by recalling the basic equations of motion under constant acceleration. Since it's vertical motion with gravity, the acceleration is -10 m/s\u00b2 because gravity acts downward.\n\nThe position as a function of time should be given by the equation:\n\ns(t) = V*t - (1/2)*g*t\u00b2\n\nWhere g is 10 m/s\u00b2 here. So substituting that in:\n\ns(t) = V*t - 5*t\u00b2\n\nWe need to find when the stone is at 10 meters, so set s(t) = 10:\n\n10 = V*t - 5*t\u00b2\n\nRearranging this equation gives:\n\n5*t\u00b2 - V*t + 10 = 0\n\nWait, no. Let's check again. If I move everything to one side:\n\nV*t - 5*t\u00b2 - 10 = 0\n\nWhich can be written as:\n\n-5*t\u00b2 + V*t - 10 = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1 to make the quadratic coefficient positive:\n\n5*t\u00b2 - V*t + 10 = 0\n\nWait, that's the same as before. So the quadratic equation is 5t\u00b2 - Vt + 10 = 0. Wait, but if we set s(t) = 10, then:\n\nVt - 5t\u00b2 = 10\n\nSo bringing all terms to the left:\n\n-5t\u00b2 + Vt - 10 = 0\n\nWhich is equivalent to 5t\u00b2 - Vt + 10 = 0 when multiplied by -1, but the quadratic equation is usually written as ax\u00b2 + bx + c = 0. So in this case, a = 5, b = -V, c = 10.\n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake here. Let me double-check.\n\nOriginal equation: s(t) = Vt - (1/2)*g*t\u00b2 = Vt - 5t\u00b2\n\nSet s(t) = 10:\n\nVt - 5t\u00b2 = 10\n\nThen rearranged:\n\n5t\u00b2 - Vt + 10 = 0. No, that's not right. Because moving 10 to the left gives Vt -5t\u00b2 -10 = 0, which is -5t\u00b2 + Vt -10 = 0. So if we write it in standard form, it's 5t\u00b2 - Vt +10 = 0 when multiplied by -1. Wait, but multiplying an equation by -1 doesn't change its roots. So perhaps it's better to write it as:\n\n5t\u00b2 - Vt +10 = 0\n\nBut actually, the original equation is Vt -5t\u00b2 =10. So perhaps it's better not to rearrange it that way. Let me think. Alternatively, maybe it's better to write it as:\n\n5t\u00b2 - Vt +10 = 0. Wait, no. Let's do it step by step.\n\nOriginal equation: Vt -5t\u00b2 = 10\n\nBring all terms to left side: Vt -5t\u00b2 -10 = 0\n\nWhich is the same as -5t\u00b2 + Vt -10 =0\n\nAlternatively, multiplying both sides by -1: 5t\u00b2 -Vt +10 =0. Either way, the quadratic equation is the same in terms of roots. The roots would be the same as for -5t\u00b2 + Vt -10 =0, just multiplied by -1. Since multiplying a quadratic by a constant doesn't change where it crosses zero. So perhaps I can use either form.\n\nBut let's just stick to the original rearrangement. Let's write:\n\n-5t\u00b2 + Vt -10 =0\n\nAlternatively, maybe better to write it as 5t\u00b2 - Vt +10 =0. Wait, but this would have a different discriminant. Wait, no. Let's see:\n\nSuppose the equation is -5t\u00b2 + Vt -10 =0. Then the discriminant is V\u00b2 -4*(-5)*(-10) = V\u00b2 -200.\n\nIf we write it as 5t\u00b2 -Vt +10 =0, then discriminant is (-V)^2 -4*5*10 = V\u00b2 - 200. Same discriminant. So roots would be:\n\nFor -5t\u00b2 + Vt -10 =0, using quadratic formula:\n\nt = [ -V \u00b1 sqrt(V\u00b2 -4*(-5)*(-10)) ]/(2*(-5)) )\n\nWait, let's compute that:\n\nt = [ -V \u00b1 sqrt(V\u00b2 - 200) ] / (-10)\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by -1:\n\nt = [ V \u2213 sqrt(V\u00b2 -200) ] /10\n\nSimilarly, for 5t\u00b2 -Vt +10 =0:\n\nt = [ V \u00b1 sqrt(V\u00b2 - 200) ] / (10)\n\nWait, but since the equations are different (multipli