val. Hence, no solution.\n\nBut wait, when V=15, the stone is at10 m at t=1 and t=2. So at V=15, t1=1 and t2=2. For V>15, t1 <1 and t2 >2. Wait, earlier calculation for V=16 gives t1\u22480.85 s and t2\u22482.348 s. So for V>15, t1 is before1 s, and t2 is between2 and when?\n\nWait, let's take V=20:\n\nt1\u22480.585 s, t2\u22483.414 s. So t2 is between2 and4 s. So for V=20, the stone reaches10 m at\u22480.585 s and\u22483.414 s. Therefore, if we consider that \"all moments\" must lie between2 and4 seconds, but the stone reaches10 m at0.585 s and3.414 s. Since one of the moments is outside the interval, it doesn't satisfy the condition.\n\nBut if we consider only the later time, t2, which is in (2,4), but the problem requires all moments to be in the interval, which includes both. Therefore, no solution.\n\nBut perhaps the problem allows for the stone to reach10 m only once in the interval (2,4). In that case, it must be that the stone is descending and passes through10 m once during that interval. However, since the stone is thrown upward, it can only reach10 m once during the descent if it was above10 m at the start of the interval. So for the stone to be above10 m at t=2, and then descends to10 m at some t>2.\n\nThe height at t=2 is s(2)=2V -20. For the stone to be above10 m at t=2, 2V -20 \u226510 \u21d2V\u226515. Then, the stone is above10 m at t=2 and will descend, crossing10 m again at t2. We need t2 <4.\n\nSo for V\u226515, find t2 such that t2 <4.\n\nThe time t2 is [V + sqrt(V\u00b2 -200)] /10 <4.\n\nWhich, as before, leads to V + sqrt(V\u00b2 -200) <40.\n\nSolving:\n\nsqrt(V\u00b2 -200) <40 -V\n\nSquare both sides:\n\nV\u00b2 -200 <1600 -80V +V\u00b2\n\nSimplify:\n\n-200 <1600 -80V\n\n-1800 < -80V\n\nV <22.5\n\nTherefore, for V in [15,22.5), the stone is above10 m at t=2 and descends to10 m at t2 <4. Therefore, in this case, the stone reaches10 m once in the interval (2,4) (at t2), and once before (at t1). But since the problem says \"all moments\", which i