\n\nThe roots of this quadratic equation are:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{V \\pm \\sqrt{V^2 - 200}}{10}\n\\]\n\n### Part A: First and Second Seconds (1 < t < 2)\nFor both roots \\( t_1 \\) and \\( t_2 \\) to lie within (1, 2):\n1. **Discriminant Condition**: \\( V^2 \\geq 200 \\Rightarrow V \\geq 10\\sqrt{2} \\approx 14.14 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n2. **Lower Root Condition**: \\( \\frac{V - \\sqrt{V^2 - 200}}{10} > 1 \\Rightarrow V < 15 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n3. **Upper Root Condition**: \\( \\frac{V + \\sqrt{V^2 - 200}}{10} < 2 \\Rightarrow V < 15 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n\n**Result**: \\( 10\\sqrt{2} \\leq V < 15 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n\n### Part B: Second and Fourth Seconds (2 < t < 4)\nFor both roots \\( t_1 \\) and \\( t_2 \\) to lie within (2, 4):\n1. **Lower Root Condition**: \\( \\frac{V - \\sqrt{V^2 - 200}}{10} > 2 \\Rightarrow V > 20 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n2. **Upper Root Condition**: \\( \\frac{V + \\sqrt{V^2 - 200}}{10} < 4 \\Rightarrow V < 22.5 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n\nHowever, solving these inequalities leads to a contradiction (\\( V > 20 \\) and \\( V < 15 \\)), making it impossible. Thus, **no values of \\( V \\) satisfy Part B**.\n\n### Final Answers\n**A)** \\( \\boxed{10\\sqrt{2} \\leq V < 15 \\, \\text{m/s}} \\)  \n**B)** \\( \\boxed{\\text{No such values of } V} \\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nThe problem involves determining the range of initial velocities \\( V \\) for a stone thrown vertically upward to consistently reach a height of \\( 10 \\) meters within a specific time interval.\n\n1. **Equation of Motion:**\n   \n   Given the equation describing the height as a function of time:\n   \\[\n   h(t) = Vt - \\frac{gt^2}{2}\n   \\]\n   where:\n   - \\( V \\) is the initial velocity,\n   - \\( g \\) is the acceleration due to gravity (\\( 10 \\, \\text{m/s}^2 \\)).\n\n2. **Setting the Height to 10 Meters:**\n   \n   To determine the times \\( t \\) at which the stone reaches \\( 10 \\) meters:\n   \\[\n   10 = Vt - 5t^2\n   \\]\n   \n   This quadratic equation can 