o (ratio of corresponding sides) is:\n\n   \\[\n   k^2 = \\frac{9}{121} \\implies k = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{121}} = \\frac{3}{11}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Finding the Ratio of Sides:**\n   Consequently, \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{QE}{BD} = \\frac{11}{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence, the ratio of \\( QE : BD \\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   QE : BD = 11 : 3\n   \\]\n\n#### Conclusion for Part (a):\n\\[\n\\boxed{QE : BD = 11 : 3}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (b): Given that the ratio of the areas of \\( \\triangle BME \\) and \\( \\triangle DQM \\) is 4, find the ratio \\( BQ : DE \\).\n\n1. **Setting up the Area Ratio:**\n   From the given data, we have:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle BME}{\\text{Area of } \\triangle DQM} = 4\n   \\]\n\n2. **Applying the same logic as in part (a):**\n   By similarity, the areas ratio is also equal to the ratio of the products of corresponding sides:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle BME}{\\text{Area of } \\triangle DQM} = \\frac{MB}{MQ} \\cdot \\frac{ME}{MD} = 4\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\( MQ = \\frac{11}{3} MD \\) and \\( ME = \\frac{11}{3} MB \\), substituting these values:\n\n   \\[\n   4 = \\frac{MB}{\\frac{11}{3} MD} \\cdot \\frac{\\frac{11}{3} MB}{MD}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplifying for \\( \\frac{MB}{MD} \\):**\n   Rearranging and simplifying,\n\n   \\[\n   4 = \\left(\\frac{MB}{MD}\\right)^2 \\implies \\frac{MB}{MD} = 2\n   \\]\n\n   Letting \\( MD = 3x \\), we find \\( MB = 6x \\).\n\n4. **Finding Remaining Sides:**\n   Thus,\n\n   \\[\n   MQ = \\frac{11}{3} MD = 11x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad ME = \\frac{11}{3} MB = 22x\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculating Segments \\( BQ \\) and \\( DE \\):**\n   Using these side lengths:\n\n   \\[\n   BQ = MQ - MB = 11x - 6x = 5x\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   DE = ME - MD = 22x - 3x = 19x\n   \\]\n\n6. **Ratio of \\( BQ : DE \\):**\n\n   \\[\n   BQ : DE = 5x : 19x = 5 : 19\n   \\]\n\n#### Conclusion for Part (b):\n\\[\n\\boxed{BQ : DE = 5 : 19}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code"