irm the ratio \\( \\frac{S_1}{S_2} = 2 \\).\n\nThus, the value of \\( \\frac{S_1}{S_2} \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven: \n\\[ 3 \\overrightarrow{A B} + 2 \\overrightarrow{B C} + \\overrightarrow{C A} = 4 \\overrightarrow{A O} \\]\n\nWe need to determine the value of \\(\\frac{S_1}{S_2}\\) where:\n- \\(S_1\\) is the area of \\(\\triangle ABC\\)\n- \\(S_2\\) is the area of \\(\\triangle OBC\\)\n\n1. Start by expressing \\(\\overrightarrow{B C}\\) in terms of \\(\\overrightarrow{C A}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{A B}\\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\overrightarrow{B C} = 2 (\\overrightarrow{A C} - \\overrightarrow{A B})\n   \\]\n   Simplify this into the original equation:\n   \\[\n   3 \\overrightarrow{A B} + 2 (\\overrightarrow{A C} - \\overrightarrow{A B}) + \\overrightarrow{C A} = 4 \\overrightarrow{A O}\n   \\]\n\n2. Distribute and combine like terms on the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   3 \\overrightarrow{A B} + 2 \\overrightarrow{A C} - 2 \\overrightarrow{A B} + \\overrightarrow{A C} = 4 \\overrightarrow{A O}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{A B} + 3 \\overrightarrow{A C} = 4 \\overrightarrow{A O}\n   \\]\n\n3. Realize that \\(\\overrightarrow{A B} + \\overrightarrow{A C} = 4 \\overrightarrow{A O}\\) by correctly aligning the remaining terms:\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{A B} + \\overrightarrow{A C} = 4 \\overrightarrow{A O}\n   \\]\n\n4. Next, let \\(D\\) be the midpoint of \\(\\overrightarrow{B C}\\). By the midpoint definition:\n   \\[\n   2 \\overrightarrow{A D} = 4 \\overrightarrow{A O}\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{A D} = 2 \\overrightarrow{A O}\n   \\]\n\n5. Since \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\( \\overrightarrow{A D}\\), the points \\(A, O, D\\) are collinear, and \\(O\\) is exactly halfway along \\(\\overrightarrow{A D}\\).\n\n6. Consequently, the triangles \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle OBC\\) share the same base \\(BC\\), and since \\(O\\) is the midpoint along \\(AD\\), the height from \\