ates, compute the Euclidean distance, but since DE is a straight line, the ratios should hold. Wait, but the problem statement says 1/PM - 1/PN = 1/PD - 1/PE. So if PM and PN are distances along DE, then maybe similar to PD and PE.\n\nBut in coordinate terms, PM is the distance from P(p, q) to M along DE. Wait, but M is the intersection of FK and DE. So M is a point on DE. Similarly, N is the intersection of HG and DE.\n\nAlternatively, in coordinates, since we have coordinates for M and N, we can compute PM and PN as the Euclidean distances from P to M and P to N.\n\nAlternatively, along DE, the positions of M and N can be parameterized, and then PM and PN can be expressed in terms of parameters.\n\nAlternatively, given that DE is the line y = -x + p + q, we can parameterize DE with a parameter s, where s = 0 corresponds to D(p + q, 0) and s = 1 corresponds to E(0, p + q). Then any point on DE can be expressed as ( (p + q)(1 - s), (p + q)s ). Then P is at s = t = q/(p + q), so P is ( (p + q)(1 - q/(p + q)), (p + q)(q/(p + q)) ) = (p, q), which checks out.\n\nThen points M and N are on DE, so their coordinates can be written as ( (p + q)(1 - s), (p + q)s ) for some s values. Then PM would be the distance along DE from P to M, which is |s - t| * length of DE. Similarly for PN. But the problem statement uses 1/PM - 1/PN. If PM and PN are directed distances, but since they are lengths, they are positive. Therefore, 1/PM - 1/PN = 1/PD - 1/PE. If PD and PE are fixed, then this relates the positions of M and N.\n\nAlternatively, using coordinates, let's compute coordinates of M and N.\n\nEarlier, we found coordinates of M as:\n\nx = [p + q - p\u00b2 - pq - q\u00b2]/(1 - p)\n\nWait, but maybe that's too messy. Let's compute coordinates of M by solving the intersection of FK and DE.\n\nWe have coordinates:\n\nF(p, 0), K(1 - q, q). The line FK can be parametrized as x = p + t(1 - q - p), y = 0 + t(q). \n\nDE is y = -x + p + q.\n\nIntersection M: set y = tq = -x + p + q, and x = p + t(1 - q - p). Substitute x into the DE equation:\n\ntq = -[p + t(1 - q - p)] + p + q\n\nSimplify:\n\ntq = -p - t(1 - q - p) + p + q\n\ntq = -t(1 - q - p) + q\n\nBring terms with t to the left:\n\ntq + t(1 - q - p) = q\n\nFactor t:\n\nt(q + 1 - q - p) = q\n\nt(1 - p) = q\n\nSo t = q/(1 - p)\n\nTherefore, coordinates of M are:\n\nx = p + (q/(1 - p))(1 - q - p) = p + q(1 - p - q)/(1 - p)\n\ny = tq = q\u00b2/(1 - p)\n\nTherefore, coordinates of M are ( p + [q(1 - p - q)]/(1 - p), q\u00b2/(1 - p) )\n\nSimilarly, let's find coordinates of N, which is the intersection of HG and DE.\n\nLine HG connects H(0, q) to G(p, 1 - p). Let's parametrize HG as x = 0 + s(p - 0) = sp, y = q + s(1 - p - q). \n\nIntersection with DE: y = -x + p + q. So,\n\nq + s(1 - p - q) = -sp + p + q\n\nSubtract q from both sides:\n\ns(1 - p - q) = -sp + p\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\ns(1 - p - q) + sp - p = 0\n\nFactor s:\n\ns(1 - p - q + p) - p = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\ns(1 - q) - p = 0\n\nTherefore, s = p / (1 - q)\n\nTherefore, coordinates of N are:\n\nx = sp = p * (p / (1 - q)) = p\u00b2 / (1 - q)\n\ny = q + s(1 - p - q) = q + (p / (1 - q))(1 - p - q)\n\nSimplify y:\n\n= q + p(1 - p - q)/(1 - q)\n\nSo coordinates of N are ( p\u00b2/(1 - q), q + p(1 - p - q)/(1 - q) )\n\nNow, PM is the distance from P(p, q) to M(p + [q(1 - p - q)]/(1 - p), q\u00b2/(1 - p)).\n\nCompute the difference in x-coordinates:\n\n\u0394x = [p + q(1 - p - q)/(1 - p)] - p = q(1 - p - q)/(1 - p)\n\n\u0394y = q\u00b2/(1 - p) - q = q\u00b2/(1 - p) - q(1 - p)/(1 - p) = [q\u00b2 - q(1 - p)] / (1 - p) = q(q - 1 + p)/(1 - p)\n\nTherefore, PM = sqrt[(\u0394x)^2 + (\u0394y)^2]\n\nSimilarly, PN is the distance from P(p, q) to N(p\u00b2/(1 - q), q + p(1 - p - q)/(1 - q)).\n\n\u0394x = p\u00b2/(1 - q) - p = p(p/(1 - q) - 1) = p(p - (1 - q))/(1 - q) = p(p + q - 1)/(1 - q)\n\n\u0394y = [q + p(1 - p - q)/(1 - q)] - q = p(1 - p - q)/(1 - q)\n\nThus, PN = sqrt[(\u0394x)^2 + (\u0394y)^2]\n\nNow, let's compute 1/PM - 1/PN and see if it equals 1/PD - 1/PE.\n\nGiven PD = q\u221a2, PE = p\u221a2, so 1/PD - 1/PE = (1/(q\u221a2) - 1/(p\u221a2)) = (1/q - 1/p)/\u221a2.\n\nLet me compute 1/PM - 1/PN.\n\nFirst, compute PM:\n\nPM^2 = [q(1 - p - q)/(1 - p)]^2 + [q(q - 1 + p)/(1 - p)]^2\n\nFactor out [q/(1 - p)]^2:\n\n= [q/(1 - p)]^2 [ (1 - p - q)^2 + (q - 1 + p)^2 ]\n\nBut notice that (q - 1 + p) = (p + q - 1) = -(1 - p - q)\n\nThus, (q - 1 + p)^2 = (1 - p - q)^2\n\nTherefore, PM^2 = [q/(1 - p)]^2 [ (1 - p - q)^2 + (1 - p - q)^2 ] = [q/(1 - p)]^2 * 2(1 - p - q)^2\n\nTherefore, PM = [q/(1 - p)] * sqrt[2] * |1 - p - q|\n\nBut since P is inside the triangle, 1 - p - q > 0 (because in triangle ABC, p + q < 1). So 1 - p - q is positive, so absolute value can be removed:\n\nPM = [q/(1 - p)] * sqrt(2) * (1 - p - q) = q(1 - p - q)sqrt(2)/(1 - p)\n\nSimilarly, compute PN:\n\nPN^2 = [p(p + q - 1)/(1 - q)]^2 + [p(1 - p - q)/(1 - q)]^2\n\nFactor out [p/(1 - q)]^2:\n\n= [p/(1 - q)]^2 [ (p + q - 1)^2 + (1 - p - q)^2 ]\n\nSimilarly, (p + q - 1)^2 = (1 - p - q)^2\n\nSo PN^2 = [p/(1 - q)]^2 * 2(1 - p - q)^2\n\nTherefore, PN = [p/(1 - q)] * sqrt(2) * |1 - p - q| = p(1 - p - q)sqrt(2)/(1 - q)\n\nThus, PM = q(1 - p - q)sqrt(2)/(1 - p)\n\nPN = p(1 - p - q)sqrt(2)/(1 - q)\n\nTherefore, 1/PM = (1 - p)/(q(1 - p - q)sqrt(2))\n\n1/PN = (1 - q)/(p(1 - p - q)sqrt(2))\n\nTherefore, 1/PM - 1/PN = [ (1 - p)/q - (1 - q)/p ] / ( (1 - p - q)sqrt(2) )\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n(1 - p)/q - (1 - q)/p = [p(1 - p) - q(1 - q)] / (pq)\n\nCompute numerator:\n\np(1 - p) - q(1 - q) = p - p\u00b2 - q + q\u00b2\n\n= (p - q) - (p\u00b2 - q\u00b2) = (p - q) - (p - q)(p + q) = (p - q)(1 - (p + q))\n\nTherefore, numerator = (p - q)(1 - p - q)\n\nThus, 1/PM - 1/PN = [ (p - q)(1 - p - q) / (pq) ] / ( (1 - p - q)sqrt(2) ) ) = (p - q)/(pq sqrt(2))\n\nBut 1/PD - 1/PE = (1/q - 1/p)/sqrt(2) = (p - q)/(pq sqrt(2))\n\nTherefore, 1/PM - 1/PN = (p - q)/(pq sqrt(2)) = 1/PD - 1/PE\n\nWhich proves statement (1).\n\nFor statement (2), if P is the midpoint of DE, then we need to show PM = PN.\n\nIf P is the midpoint of DE, then in the parameterization along DE, P is halfway between D and E. In our earlier parameterization, t = q/(p + q) = 1/2, so q = (p + q)/2 => 2q = p + q => q = p. Therefore, p = q.\n\nSo if P is the midpoint, then p = q. Let's substitute p = q into expressions for PM and PN.\n\nFrom earlier:\n\nPM = q(1 - p - q)sqrt(2)/(1 - p)\n\nBut p = q, so:\n\nPM = p(1 - 2p)sqrt(2)/(1 - p)\n\nSimilarly, PN = p(1 - 2p)sqrt(2)/(1 - p)\n\nWait, but substituting p = q into PN:\n\nPN = p(1 - p - q)sqrt(2)/(1 - q) = p(1 - 2p)sqrt(2)/(1 - p)\n\nWhich is the same as PM. Therefore, PM = PN when p = q, i.e., when P is the midpoint of DE.\n\nHence, statement (2) is proven.\n\nTherefore, both statements (1) and (2) hold true based on the coordinate analysis.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\nBoth statements (1) and (2) are proven to be true. The final answers are:\n\n(1) \\boxed{\\frac{1}{PM} - \\frac{1}{PN} = \\frac{1}{PD} - \\frac{1}{PE}}\n\n(2) \\boxed{PM = PN}", "deepseek_solution": "Given triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) with a point \\( P \\) inside, lines \\( DE \\) and \\( FG \\) are drawn parallel to \\( BC \\) and \\( AC \\) respectively, intersecting lines \\( AB \\) and \\( AC \\) at points \\( D \\) and \\( E \\), and \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\) at points \\( F \\) and \\( G \\). Line \\( HK \\) is parallel to \\( AB \\), intersecting lines \\( AC \\) and \\( BC \\) at points \\( H \\) and \\( K \\). Lines \\( FK \\) and \\( HG \\) intersect line \\( DE \\) at points \\( M \\) and \\( N \\).\n\n### Key Steps and Analysis:\n\n1. **Coordinate System Setup**:\n   - Place \\( A \\) at \\( (0, 0) \\), \\( B \\) at \\( (1, 0) \\), and \\( C \\) at \\( (0, 1) \\).\n   - Assume \\( P \\) has coordinates \\( (p, q) \\).\n\n2. **Equations of Lines**:\n   - Line \\( DE \\) (parallel to \\( BC \\)) through \\( P \\): \\( y = -x + p + q \\).\n   - Line \\( FG \\) (parallel to \\( AC \\)) through \\( P \\): \\( x = p \\).\n   - Line \\( HK \\) (parallel to \\( AB \\)) intersecting \\( AC \\) at \\( H(0, q) \\) and \\( BC \\) at \\( K(1 - q, q) \\).\n\n3. **Intersection Points**:\n   - Coordinates of \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) on \\( AB \\) and \\( AC \\): \\( D(p + q, 0) \\) and \\( E(0, p + q) \\).\n   - Coordinates of \\( F \\) and \\( G \\) on \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\): \\( F(p, 0) \\) and \\( G(p, 1 - p) \\).\n\n4. **Finding \\( M \\) and \\( N \\)**:\n   - **Line \\( FK \\)**: Parametrized from \\( F(p, 0) \\) to \\( K(1 - q, q) \\). Intersection with \\( DE \\) gives \\( M \\).\n   - **Line \\( HG \\)**: Parametrized from \\( H(0, q) \\) to \\( G(p, 1 - p) \\). Intersection with \\( DE \\) gives \\( N \\).\n\n5. **Distance Calculations**:\n   - **Distances \\( PD \\) and \\( PE \\)**: Euclidean distances from \\( P \\) to \\( D \\) and \\( E \\).\n   - **Distances \\( PM \\) and \\( PN \\)**: Euclidean distances from \\( P \\) to \\( M \\) and \\( N \\).\n\n6. **Proof of Statements**:\n   - **Statement (1)**: Using coordinate analysis, it is shown that \\( \\frac{1}{PM} - \\frac{1}{PN} = \\frac{1}{PD} - \\frac{1}{PE} \\).\n   - **Statement (2)**: When \\( P \\) is the midpoint of \\( DE \\), \\( p = q \\), leading to \\( PM = PN \\).\n\n### Final Answers\n\n(1) \\boxed{\\frac{1}{PM} - \\frac{1}{PN} = \\frac{1}{PD} - \\frac{1}{PE}}\n\n(2) \\boxed{PM = PN}", "ground_truth_solution": "Given the geometric arrangement, let's prove the two parts of the problem as follows:\n\n#### Part (1):\n\nConsider the triangles and the lines as depicted in the problem statement. \n\n1. In $\\triangle FBG$ and $\\triangle FKG$, since $DE \\parallel BC$, we have:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{PD}{BG} = \\frac{FP}{FG} = \\frac{PM}{KG}\n    \\]\n\n2. This implies:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{PD}{BK + KG} = \\frac{PM}{KG}\n    \\]\n\n3. Since $BK = PD$ (from the properties of parallel lines and similar triangles), we substitute to get:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{PD}{PD + KG} = \\frac{PM}{KG}\n    \\]\n\n4. Solving for $KG$ in terms of $PD$ and $PM$, we get:\n    \\[\n    KG = \\frac{PM \\cdot PD}{PD - PM}\n    \\]\n\n5. Similarly, in $\\triangle HKC$ and $\\triangle HKG$, where $KG \\parallel AC$, we can show:\n    \\[\n    KG = \\frac{PN \\cdot PE}{PE - PN}\n    \\]\n\n6. Equate the two expressions for $KG$:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{PM \\cdot PD}{PD - PM} = \\frac{PN \\cdot PE}{PE - PN}\n    \\]\n\n7. Cross-multiplying and simplifying the equation, we get:\n    \\[\n    PM \\cdot PD \\cdot (PE - PN) = PN \\cdot PE \\cdot (PD - PM)\n    \\]\n\n8. Dividing both sides by $PM \\cdot PN \\cdot PD \\cdot PE$ to isolate the terms:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{PM} - \\frac{1}{PN} = \\frac{1}{PD} - \\frac{1}{PE}\n    \\]\n\nThus, we have proven that:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{PM} - \\frac{1}{PN} = \\frac{1}{PD} - \\frac{1}{PE}}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (2):\n\nGiven that $P$ is the midpoint of $DE$:\n\n1. If $P$ is the midpoint, then:\n    \\[\n    PD = PE\n    \\]\n\n2. Applying the result from Part (1):\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{PM} - \\frac{1}{PN} = \\frac{1}{PD} - \\frac{1}{PE}\n    \\]\n\n3. As $PD = PE$, the right-hand side becomes zero:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{PM} - \\frac{1}{PN} = 0\n    \\]\n\n4. This implies:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{PM} = \\frac{1}{PN}\n    \\]\n\n5. Therefore, \n    \\[\n    PM = PN\n    \\]\n\nHence, if $P$ is the midpoint of $DE$, we conclude:\n\\[\n\\boxed{PM = PN}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find all prime numbers \\( p \\) such that \\( p^2 + 14 \\) is also a prime number.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all prime numbers p where p squared plus 14 is also a prime number. Hmm, let's start by understanding the problem. We're dealing with primes, so primes are numbers greater than 1 that have no divisors other than 1 and themselves. The task is to find primes p such that when you square p and add 14, the result is still prime. \n\nFirst, maybe I should start by testing small prime numbers and see if the condition holds. That might give me some pattern or idea. The primes start at 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, and so on. Let's check each one.\n\nStarting with p = 2. Then p\u00b2 + 14 would be 4 + 14 = 18. Is 18 a prime? No, because 18 is divisible by 2, 3, 6, 9. So 2 doesn't work.\n\nNext, p = 3. Then 3\u00b2 +14 = 9 +14 = 23. 23 is a prime number. So 3 is a candidate.\n\nThen p = 5. 5 squared is 25, plus 14 is 39. 39 is 3 times 13, so not prime. So 5 doesn't work.\n\np = 7. 7\u00b2 = 49 +14 = 63. 63 is 7*9, so definitely not prime. So 7 is out.\n\nNext prime is 11. 11\u00b2 = 121 +14 = 135. 135 is divisible by 5 (since it ends with 5) and 5*27=135. Not prime.\n\np = 13. 13\u00b2 = 169 +14 = 183. Let's check 183. 1+8+3=12, which is divisible by 3, so 183 is divisible by 3. 3*61=183, so not prime.\n\np = 17. 17\u00b2 = 289 +14 = 303. 303 divided by 3? 3*101=303. So not prime.\n\np = 19. 19\u00b2 = 361 +14 = 375. Ends with 5, so divisible by 5. Not prime.\n\np = 23. 23\u00b2 = 529 +14 = 543. 5+4+3=12, divisible by 3. So 543 is divisible by 3. Not prime.\n\np = 29. 29\u00b2 = 841 +14 = 855. Ends with 5, divisible by 5. Not prime.\n\nHmm, so far only p=3 works. Let me check a few more primes just to be thorough.\n\np=31. 31\u00b2=961+14=975. Ends with 5, divisible by 5. Not prime.\n\np=37. 37\u00b2=1369+14=1383. Check divisibility. 1+3+8+3=15, divisible by 3. So 1383/3=461. Not prime.\n\nWait, maybe there's a pattern here. For primes larger than 3, p\u00b2 +14 seems to be divisible by 3. Let me think about that. Maybe modular arithmetic can help here. If we consider primes p >3, all primes except 2 and 3 are of the form 6k\u00b11. So maybe p can be expressed as 6k\u00b11. Let's compute p\u00b2 modulo 3. \n\nIf p is a prime greater than 3, then p cannot be divisible by 3. Therefore, p is congruent to either 1 or 2 mod 3. Let's compute p\u00b2 modulo 3. If p \u22611 mod3, then p\u00b2 \u22611\u00b2=1 mod3. If p \u22612 mod3, then p\u00b2 \u22614\u22611 mod3. So in either case, p\u00b2 \u22611 mod3. Therefore, p\u00b2 +14 \u22611 +14 \u226115\u22610 mod3. Therefore, p\u00b2 +14 is divisible by 3. But p\u00b2 +14 is supposed to be prime. The only way that p\u00b2 +14 is divisible by 3 and prime is if p\u00b2 +14 itself is 3. But 3 is the only prime divisible by 3. So p\u00b2 +14=3 implies p\u00b2= -11, which is impossible. Therefore, for any prime p>3, p\u00b2 +14 is divisible by 3 and greater than 3, hence composite. \n\nTherefore, the only possible primes p are 2 and 3. Wait, but when we checked p=2 earlier, p\u00b2 +14=18, which is not prime. So only p=3 works. \n\nWait, but let's confirm this reasoning. Since all primes except 2 and 3 are congruent to 1 or 2 mod3, so their squares are 1 mod3. Then p\u00b2 +14 \u22611 + 14 =15\u22610 mod3. Therefore, p\u00b2 +14 is divisible by 3. If p is 3 itself, let's check that. p=3, p\u00b2+14=9+14=23, which is prime. So 3 is allowed. But for any other prime, since p is not 3, p\u00b2 +14 is divisible by 3 and greater than 3, hence composite. \n\nTherefore, the only possible prime is p=3. \n\nBut let me just verify p=2 again. 2 is a prime, 2\u00b2 +14=4+14=18, which is not prime. So p=2 is excluded. \n\nSo conclusion is only p=3. \n\nBut let's double-check with another approach. Suppose we think of even primes. The only even prime is 2. So check p=2: 18, not prime. All other primes are odd. So p is odd, so p\u00b2 is odd, 14 is even. So p\u00b2 +14 is odd + even = odd. So primes can be odd, so that's okay. But the key is the modulus 3 argument. \n\nAnother way: For primes p>3, since they are not divisible by 3, their squares mod3 are 1, as shown. So p\u00b2 +14 \u22610 mod3, so divisible by 3. Therefore, the only possible primes are p=2 and p=3. But p=2 gives 18, which is not prime. So only p=3. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is p=3.", "deepseek_solution": "To find all prime numbers \\( p \\) such that \\( p^2 + 14 \\) is also prime, we analyze the problem systematically:\n\n1. **Testing Small Primes**:\n   - For \\( p = 2 \\): \\( 2^2 + 14 = 18 \\) (not prime).\n   - For \\( p = 3 \\): \\( 3^2 + 14 = 23 \\) (prime).\n   - For \\( p = 5, 7, 11, 13, \\ldots \\): All results \\( (39, 63, 135, 183, \\ldots) \\) are composite.\n\n2. **Modular Arithmetic Insight**:\n   - For primes \\( p > 3 \\), \\( p \\equiv \\pm1 \\mod 3 \\). Thus, \\( p^2 \\equiv 1 \\mod 3 \\).\n   - Adding 14: \\( p^2 + 14 \\equiv 1 + 14 \\equiv 15 \\equiv 0 \\mod 3 \\), making \\( p^2 + 14 \\) divisible by 3 and hence composite (since \\( p^2 + 14 > 3 \\)).\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Only \\( p = 3 \\) satisfies the condition, as \\( 3^2 + 14 = 23 \\) is prime.\n   - All other primes \\( p > 3 \\) result in \\( p^2 + 14 \\) being composite.\n\n**Final Answer**: The only prime number \\( p \\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Check for Small Values of \\( p \\)**:\n    - Let us start by checking if \\( p = 3 \\) is a solution.\n    - Compute \\( p^2 + 14 \\) when \\( p = 3 \\):\n        \\[\n        p^2 + 14 = 3^2 + 14 = 9 + 14 = 23\n        \\]\n    - Since 23 is a prime number, \\( p = 3 \\) satisfies the condition.\n\n2. **Analyze General Case \\( p \\neq 3 \\)**:\n    - For \\( p \\neq 3 \\), we examine the form of \\( p^2 \\).\n    - Since \\( p \\) is a prime and \\( p \\neq 3 \\), we can claim \\( p \\) is not divisible by 3.\n    - Consequently, \\( p^2 \\) can take one of the forms based on modular arithmetic [using Modulo 3 properties]:\n        \\[\n        p^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n        \\]\n    - Let us denote \\( p^2 \\) as:\n        \\[\n        p^2 = 3k + 1 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n        \\]\n\n3. **Simplify \\( p^2 + 14 \\)**:\n    - Substitute \\( p^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\) into the expression for \\( p^2 + 14 \\):\n        \\[\n        p^2 + 14 \\equiv 1 + 14 \\equiv 15 \\pmod{3}\n        \\]\n    - Notice that \\( 15 = 3 \\cdot 5 \\), hence \\( 15 \\) is divisible by 3:\n        \\[\n        p^2 + 14 = 3k + 15 = 3(k + 5)\n        \\]\n\n4. **Conclude the Composite Nature**:\n    - Since \\( p^2 + 14 \\) is divisible by 3 for \\( p \\neq 3 \\), it implies that \\( p^2 + 14 \\) is a composite number in cases where \\( p \\neq 3 \\).\n    - Therefore, no other primes can satisfy the condition other than \\( p = 3 \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, the only prime number \\( p \\) where \\( p^2 + 14 \\) remains a prime number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(ABCD\\) be a convex quadrilateral such that its diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\) are perpendicular and intersect at \\(E\\). Show that the reflections of \\(E\\) with respect to the four sides of the quadrilateral lie on a single circle.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this geometry problem here: Let ABCD be a convex quadrilateral with perpendicular diagonals AC and BD intersecting at point E. I need to show that the reflections of E over the four sides of the quadrilateral all lie on a single circle. Hmm, interesting. Let me think through this step by step.\n\nFirst, let me recall some basic properties about reflections in geometry. When you reflect a point over a line, the line is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the original point and its reflection. So, if I reflect E over a side of the quadrilateral, say AB, the line AB will be the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting E and its reflection, which I'll call E_AB. The same applies for the other sides: BC, CD, and DA. So, each reflection point will be such that the respective side is the perpendicular bisector between E and its reflection.\n\nNow, the problem states that the diagonals AC and BD are perpendicular and intersect at E. That's a crucial piece of information. Maybe I can use coordinate geometry here? Setting up coordinates might help visualize or compute the positions of the reflections.\n\nLet me try to assign coordinates to the quadrilateral. Since the diagonals are perpen