/5\n\nTherefore, for x < 0, the inequality \\(5 + \\frac{2}{x} > 0\\) holds when x < -2/5.\n\nSo combining both cases, the solution for the second logarithm is x \u2208 (-\u221e, -2/5) \u222a (0, \u221e).\n\nBut wait, the original argument was \\(5 + \\frac{2}{x} > 0\\). Let me check with x = -1 (which is less than -2/5):\n\n5 + 2/(-1) = 5 - 2 = 3 > 0, which works.\n\nx = -0.3 (between -2/5 \u2248 -0.4 and 0):\n\n5 + 2/(-0.3) \u2248 5 - 6.666... \u2248 -1.666 < 0, which doesn't work.\n\nx = 0.5:\n\n5 + 2/0.5 = 5 + 4 = 9 > 0, which works.\n\nSo yes, the domain for the second logarithm is x \u2208 (-\u221e, -2/5) \u222a (0, \u221e).\n\nFinally, the right-hand side logarithm: \\(\\log_{5}(5x^{2})\\). The argument here is 5x\u00b2, which is always positive as long as x \u2260 0 (since x\u00b2 is non-negative, multiplied by 5 gives positive unless x=0). Therefore, the domain here is x \u2260 0.\n\nNow, combining all three domains:\n\nFirst log: x \u2208 (-\u221e, -2/5) \u222a (0, \u221e)\n\nSecond log: same as first\n\nThird log: x \u2260 0\n\nSo the overall domain is x \u2208 (-\u221e, -2/5) \u222a (0, \u221e).\n\nTherefore, the solution must lie within this domain.\n\nNow, moving on to the inequality itself:\n\n\\(\\log _{5}(5x^{2} + 2x) \\cdot \\log _{5}(5 + \\frac{2}{x}) > \\log _{5}(5x^{2})\\)\n\nLet me see if I can simplify or manipulate this inequality. Maybe express all terms in terms of log base 5. Let's denote A = log5(5x\u00b2 + 2x), B = log5(5 + 2/x), and C = log5(5x\u00b2). The inequality is A*B > C.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using logarithmic identities to combine terms. For example, log(a) * log(b) is not straightforward, but maybe if we can express A and B in terms that relate to C.\n\nAlternatively, let's note that 5 + 2/x might be related to (5x\u00b2 + 2x)/x\u00b2. Let me check:\n\n(5x\u00b2 + 2x)/x\u00b2 = 5 + 2/x. Oh, that's exactly the argument of the second logarithm!\n\nSo, 5 + 2/x = (5x\u00b2 + 2x)/x\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, \\(\\log_{5}(5 + 2/x) = \