c{\\sqrt{2} - 1}{5}, \\infty\\right)}.\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nConsider the inequality:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{5} \\left(5x^{2} + 2x\\right) \\cdot \\log_{5} \\left(5 + \\frac{2}{x}\\right) > \\log_{5} 5x^{2}\n\\]\n\n1. **Simplify the inequality**:\n   \\[\n   \\log_{5} \\left(5 + \\frac{2}{x}\\right) \\cdot \\log_{5} \\left(5x^{2} + 2x\\right) > \\log_{5} 5x^{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Using properties of logarithms, \\(\\log_{5}(ab) = \\log_{5}a + \\log_{5}b\\):\n   \\[\n   \\log_{5} \\left(5x^{2} + 2x\\right) = \\log_{5} \\left(5 \\cdot x^2 (1 + \\frac{2}{5x})\\right) = \\log_{5}\\left(5 + \\frac{2}{x}\\right) + \\log_{5}x^2 + \\log_{5}5\n   \\]\n\n2. **Rearrange and further simplify**:\n   \\[\n   \\log_{5} \\left(5 + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\left(\\log_{5} \\left(5x^{2} + 2x\\right)\\right)> \\log_{5} 5x^{2} = \\log_{5}5 + \\log_{5}x^2 = 1 + 2\\log_{5}x\n   \\]\n\n3. **Rewriting the inequality**:\n   \\[\n   \\log_{5} \\left(5+ \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\left(\\log_{5} \\left(5+ \\frac{2}{x}\\right) + \\log_{5} x^{2} \\right) > 1 + \\log_{5} x^{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Let }\\ y = \\log_{5}\\left(5 + \\frac{2}{x}\\right). \\text{ Then}: \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y \\cdot \\left(y + 2\\log_{5}x \\right) > 1 + 2\\log_{5}x\n   \\]\n\n4. **Group terms and isolate the product**:\n   \\[\n   y^{2} + y \\cdot 2 \\log_{5} x > 1 + 2 \\log_{5}x\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y^2 + 2y \\log_{5} x - 1 - 2\\log_{5} x > 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (y - 1)(y + 2\\log_{5} x + 1) > 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Critical points and intervals**:\n\n   The critical points are when:\n   \\[\n   y - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad (y + 2 \\log_{5} x + 1) = 0\n   \\]\n\n   We need to solve:\n   \\[\n   5 + \\frac{2}{x} = 5 \\Rightarrow \\frac{2}{x} = 0 \\quad \\text{(no solution as $x \\neq 0$) and}\n   \\]\n\n   - For \\((y - 1)(y + 2\\log_{5}x + 1) > 0\\):\n\n6. **Establish cases and solve**:\n\n   \\[\n   5 + \\frac{2}{x} > 5 \\Rightarrow x < - \\frac{2}{5} \\quad \\text{or } x > 0\n   \\]\n\n7. **Evaluate intervals**:\n\n  