81 is x\u00b2 + (y - k)\u00b2 = k\u00b2 \u21d2 x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 - 2ky = 0.\n\nPoint D is on S\u2081 and on line y = q. So substituting y = q into S\u2081's equation: x\u00b2 + q\u00b2 - 2kq = 0 \u21d2 x\u00b2 = 2kq - q\u00b2. Therefore, the x-coordinates of D are \u00b1\u221a(2kq - q\u00b2). Assuming D is distinct from A, which is at (0,0), then q \u2260 0, so 2kq - q\u00b2 > 0 \u21d2 q(2k - q) > 0. So, depending on the sign of q, but since AB is a tangent, and S\u2081 is above or below A.\n\nWait, since OA is vertical and AB is horizontal tangent at A(0,0), the center of S\u2081 is at (0, k), either above or below. If AB is the tangent at A, and AB is along the x-axis, then the radius OA is along the y-axis. If the center is at (0, k), then k must be positive or negative. Let's assume k > 0, so center is above A. Then, the tangent at A is the x-axis, and circle S\u2081 is above the x-axis. Then, point P is another intersection of S\u2081 and S\u2082.\n\nBut wait, S\u2081 and S\u2082 intersect at A and P. So, point P is also on S\u2081, so its coordinates satisfy x\u00b2 + (y - k)^2 = k^2. So, x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 - 2ky = 0. And P is also on S\u2082.\n\nS\u2082 passes through points A(0,0), B(b, 0), P(p, q), and C(c_x, q) since C is on S\u2082 and line CD (y = q). Wait, line CD is horizontal at y = q, passing through P(p, q), so point C is (c, q), and D is (d, q). But D is on S\u2081, so d\u00b2 + q\u00b2 - 2kq = 0 \u21d2 d = \u00b1\u221a(2kq - q\u00b2). Let's say D is (d, q).\n\nNow, since C is on S\u2082 and line y = q, so C is (c, q). So, we need to find coordinates of C and D such that S\u2082 passes through A(0,0), B(b,0), P(p,q), and C(c,q).\n\nGiven four points on S\u2082, let's find the equation of S\u2082. The general equation of a circle is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0.\n\nSince A(0,0) is on S\u2082: 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + c = 0 \u21d2 c = 0.\n\nSo, equation of S\u2082 is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + 2gx + 2fy = 0.\n\nPoint B(b,0) is on S\u2082: b\u00b2 + 0 + 2g*b 