, (1,2) is losing because all moves lead to winning positions.\n\n- (1,1): can move right to (1,2) (losing) or up to (2,1) (winning). Therefore, (1,1) is winning because it can move to a losing position (1,2).\n\nTherefore, the starting position (1,1) is a winning position. Therefore, Jacob can win by moving to (1,2), which is a losing position for Pavel. Once Pavel is at (1,2), any move he makes will lead to a winning position for Jacob. For example, if Pavel moves from (1,2) to (1,3), Jacob can move up to (2,3) and win. If Pavel moves from (1,2) to (2,2), Jacob can move right to (2,3) and win. Therefore, yes, (1,2) is a losing position, so Jacob can force a win by moving there.\n\nThis aligns with the Grundy number analysis. So the key is to determine for each position whether it's winning or losing, then the starting position (1,1) will be winning or losing accordingly.\n\nSo, to generalize, we can model this as a grid where each cell (i,j) is a position, and we need to compute whether it's a winning or losing position. The recurrence relation is:\n\n- A position (i,j) is winning if there exists a move to a losing position.\n\n- A position (i,j) is losing if all moves from (i,j) lead to winning positions.\n\nThe base case is the terminal position (m,n), which we can consider as a losing position because no moves can be made from it (even though reaching it is a win; this part is tricky). Wait, actually, if (m,n) is the terminal position, you can't move from it, so it's losing. But reaching it is a win. Therefore, positions that can move to (m,n) are winning. Then positions that can only move to winning positions are losing, etc.\n\nThis seems like the standard approach. Therefore, we can build a table for small m and n to find a pattern.\n\nLet's attempt to compute a few more positions.\n\nFor a 3x4 grid:\n\nTerminal position: (3,4). It's losing.\n\nPositions that can reach (3,4) in one move:\n\n- (3,3): move right 1.\n\n- (2,4): move up 1.\n\nSo (3,3) and (2,4) 